WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.720
Our company is now Meta. When you hear a tech&nbsp;
buzzword like “the Metaverse” it’s easy to tune it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.480
out as just another marketing stunt. But I think&nbsp;
it actually represents a really important vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.360
for the future of technology. You can’t get too&nbsp;
hung-up on the word “metaverse” though, since we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.880
probably won’t be using that term for long. After&nbsp;
all, do you know anyone who refers to the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.960
as “the information superhighway”? Obviously not,&nbsp;
but many of the predictions about the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:34.080
superhighway were surprisingly accurate. And&nbsp;
I think that we will eventually see the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.440
thing with the metaverse, but the real question is&nbsp;
when? When will VR actually be good enough to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:44.640
for more than a few hours at a time? When will the&nbsp;
average person start caring about digital goods?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.680
And when will computers become powerful enough&nbsp;
to finally create a persistent virtual world?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.640
These are tough questions, and it’s not as simple&nbsp;
as just looking at a particular company's roadmap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.480
and estimating when they will ship a&nbsp;
specific product. We’re talking about an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.240
era, so it’s best to think about the arrival of&nbsp;
the metaverse in terms of a shift in behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.600
Just like the internet is not a single place, but&nbsp;
instead a collection of websites, applications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.720
and services that work together, the metaverse&nbsp;
just describes a collection of technologies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.440
business models, and interactions that will&nbsp;
look very different from today’s internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.760
If we want to try and guess when the metaverse&nbsp;
will arrive, we’ll need to pinpoint the moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.280
at which the digital world becomes more meaningful&nbsp;
to most people than the physical world. Artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.080
intelligence researchers use the concept of&nbsp;
the singularity to describe a moment when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:39.680
technological growth becomes uncontrollable and&nbsp;
irreversible. Essentially, once an AI system can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.720
improve itself beyond human capabilities, these&nbsp;
improvements will start compounding endlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.600
and create a superintelligence that surpasses&nbsp;
all of humanity. That’s still a long way off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.840
but if you look at how people interact with the&nbsp;
digital world, it feels like the metaverse moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.640
could be right around the corner. Our focus on&nbsp;
the physical world has been declining for decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.440
Work has moved from conference rooms to video&nbsp;
calls. Friendships are increasingly formed online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.640
through Twitter and Discord communities. And video&nbsp;
games are quickly replacing sports as the dominant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.080
competitive activity. This trend is more than just&nbsp;
our activities though, it’s now about identity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:27.520
individualism. There are lots of people that&nbsp;
care deeply about their online personas, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.880
look at any instagram influencer and you’ll see&nbsp;
a perfectly curated timeline of events portraying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.920
an idealized lifestyle. New technologies have&nbsp;
been shifting our attention for decades now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.880
The physical world used to be our only option,&nbsp;
but then things started to change. TVs pulled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.600
a few hours of our attention, then computers&nbsp;
took more, and then phones allowed us to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.600
the digital world with us anywhere we went. There&nbsp;
are currently 4.6 billion internet users globally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.600
that’s more than half of the people on&nbsp;
earth. We’re firmly in the internet age now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.520
but only a small fraction of those people&nbsp;
are fully immersed in the digital world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.880
A few weeks ago, a crypto-founder named Richard&nbsp;
turned down a $9.5 million dollar offer for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.520
Cryptopunk NFT. Even though that is a staggering&nbsp;
amount of money for a single profile picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.640
Richard said that the image was now tied to his&nbsp;
identity and simply wasn’t for sale. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.600
perfect example of someone who has already crossed&nbsp;
over into metaverse territory. This might sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.560
terrible and dystopian to you, or it might sound&nbsp;
amazing and futuristic. The good news is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.200
you’ll have a choice. There are still 3 billion&nbsp;
people who don’t use the internet after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.160
and plenty of people choose to deactivate their&nbsp;
Facebook accounts every day. Regardless of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:41.440
the idea of moving into an even more online world&nbsp;
makes you feel, it’s still worthwhile to try and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.000
predict when this might happen. When will we&nbsp;
see billions of people fully immerse themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.440
in digital spaces? In order to estimate when it&nbsp;
will be apparent that the metaverse has arrived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.160
we have to lay some ground rules, because I’ve&nbsp;
seen a lot of heated debates on this topic. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.120
we need to agree that the metaverse describes a&nbsp;
future state and isn’t already here. I’ve seen a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.680
few people argue that Second Life or Roblox&nbsp;
are already metaverses and we should just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.920
stop the discussion there. It’s great to look at&nbsp;
these two games as examples of what’s possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.640
but neither are fully immersive. The Metaverse&nbsp;
will obviously build on top of what Second Life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.520
and Roblox have done, and I wouldn’t be surprised&nbsp;
if Roblox in particular plays an essential role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.080
in the metaverse going forward. But it’s just not&nbsp;
fair to say that the metaverse is already here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.760
call it a day. After all, we had cell phones in&nbsp;
the 1980s, but no one claims that the mobile era&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.640
started then. The average person in the 80s didn’t&nbsp;
even have a laptop, and real smartphone innovation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:39.520
wouldn’t pick up until the mid-2000s. We also&nbsp;
need to agree that the metaverse is possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.240
and will only be successful if people actually&nbsp;
like it. Mark Zuckerberg isn’t exactly the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.360
popular guy in tech these days, and plenty of his&nbsp;
detractors have said that they would never use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.080
products created by Facebook, or the rebranded&nbsp;
Meta. That’s a totally fine position to have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:58.720
consumers will always have particular brand&nbsp;
preferences, just look at all the Android&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:04.560
versus iPhone debates. If anything, the rivalry&nbsp;
between the two major mobile operating systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.520
actually helped phones become ubiquitous. No one&nbsp;
could stop talking about which phone was best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.360
which drew a lot of attention. The metaverse&nbsp;
will be bigger than any one company and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.080
Zuckerberg recognizes this. We’re talking&nbsp;
about a entirely new computing era here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.040
not just a particular product or service. Making&nbsp;
the metaverse a reality will require a collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.920
of technological advances. Virtual reality&nbsp;
and augmented reality have to get better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.000
systems and networks need to start talking to one&nbsp;
another, and the internet clearly needs to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.400
faster. But all of that is possible. We’re not&nbsp;
talking about crazy brain computing interfaces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.280
here like Elon Musk’s Neurolink or plugging into&nbsp;
something that perfectly recreates The Matrix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:45.440
All of the ideas laid out in Ready Player One&nbsp;
seem pretty feasible to me, even if it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.320
take quite some time to get there. So the Oasis&nbsp;
serves as a pretty good point of reference. Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.560
take a minute to focus on just the hardware&nbsp;
requirements of the Metaverse. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.040
there will be lots of different ways to interface&nbsp;
with virtual environments, but I still think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:04.160
virtual reality headsets are absolutely critical&nbsp;
here. From a casual observer’s perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:09.520
it can look like VR development has really&nbsp;
stalled out over the past few years. In 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:15.600
everyone in tech obsessed over the original Oculus&nbsp;
Rift. Palmer Luckey was the perfect founder and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.560
quickly became the poster boy for VR, all while&nbsp;
releasing dramatic improvements at a breakneck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:25.760
pace. Fast forward to 2021 and Facebook is&nbsp;
still releasing new versions of the Oculus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.960
but each iteration feels more incremental. They’ve&nbsp;
only sold a few million of them, which is nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.440
compared to the nearly 3 billion people who use&nbsp;
Facebook. But it’s important to understand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:40.720
Zuckerberg has a very particular strategy here.&nbsp;
Because he’s targeting such a large audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:46.000
he really isn’t working on shipping ultra-high-end&nbsp;
headsets to the general public. But fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:51.440
other companies are. The Varjo XR-3 is a great&nbsp;
example of what’s possible in VR when money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.240
isn’t an issue. The headset costs six thousand&nbsp;
dollars upfront and requires a fifteen hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.280
dollar per year subscription on top of that.&nbsp;
It’s not made with average consumers in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:05.920
but it’s a great demonstration of where this&nbsp;
technology is going. The first consumer Oculus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:11.680
launched in 2016 and has a resolution of a little&nbsp;
over one megapixel per eye, which wasn’t terrible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:17.440
but it led to some clearly noticeable pixelation.&nbsp;
The Oculus Quest 2, which came out last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:22.320
serves up about three and a half megapixels&nbsp;
per eye, which is roughly equivalent to 4K,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:28.960
but even that isn’t enough for true immersion.&nbsp;
The Varjo XR-3 pushes well beyond this though, by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:34.000
using multiple screens stacked on top of each&nbsp;
other to deliver more than 11 megapixels per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:38.240
eye. These aren’t perfect apples-to-apples&nbsp;
comparisons, since there are tons of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:42.800
factors that go into making a VR experience&nbsp;
feel truly immersive, but it’s a solid step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:47.520
forward. Just watch the reaction of this VR&nbsp;
enthusiast: “I don’t even know what to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:55.840
everything looks so clear and so real. You have&nbsp;
to try this.” This Varjo headset has lidar sensors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:00.080
and can be used for augmented reality as&nbsp;
well. It also improves on field of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:05.360
which is a huge part of a good experience. The&nbsp;
display of the second Hololens from Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:12.560
covers only 52 degrees, and Snap’s new AR glasses&nbsp;
are only 26 degrees. This Varjo headset delivers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:17.840
115 degrees of view, meaning you can see more&nbsp;
of your surroundings in your peripheral vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.960
which is key to immersion. Obviously the average&nbsp;
consumer isn’t going to spend thousands of dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:27.920
on a VR headset anytime soon, but this&nbsp;
tech will get cheaper, and when it does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:32.400
we should see adoption start to climb. There&nbsp;
are also a host of other technologies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.880
need to improve before the metaverse really starts&nbsp;
pulling people in. Even though Zuckerberg painted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:42.560
an incredibly optimistic picture of what this new&nbsp;
focus might accomplish, it was John Carmack, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:48.000
Chief Technology Officer of Oculus that actually&nbsp;
had the most valuable presentation during the Meta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:53.520
announcement. Even though Carmack works directly&nbsp;
for Zuckerberg, he was very sober in his analysis:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:58.326
“I have been pretty actively arguing against&nbsp;
every single metaverse effort that we have tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.326 --> 00:08:59.200
to spin up internally in the company from even&nbsp;
pre-acquisition times i am you know i want it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.960
to exist but i have pretty good reasons to believe&nbsp;
that go setting out to build the metaverse is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:09.200
actually the best way to wind up with the&nbsp;
metaverse.” That’s an interesting perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:14.080
especially coming from someone who’s been&nbsp;
working at Facebook in VR for the last 7 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:19.360
but it makes sense. Because the metaverse is&nbsp;
such a broad amalgamation of technologies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:24.320
it’s impossible for any one company&nbsp;
to usher in the metaverse era alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:29.120
But that doesn’t mean that a single company can’t&nbsp;
have a profound impact here, and make a boatload&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.480
of money in the process. I believe that Zuckerberg&nbsp;
is basically trying to be the next Steve Jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:40.160
and to do it, he needs his iPhone moment. The&nbsp;
iPhone was a major turning point in ushering in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:44.560
the mobile era. There were tons of other companies&nbsp;
that played important roles, but it was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.200
the iPhone that bought everything together and&nbsp;
served as the key turning point. And the history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:54.240
of Apple is really important here. In the 1980s,&nbsp;
the Macintosh was struggling to compete with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:10:00.960
IBM PC and so the board of directors fired Jobs.&nbsp;
He eventually rejoined Apple in 1997 and started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:06.320
working on new projects. Finally in 2007, he&nbsp;
announced the iPhone at the end of a keynote using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:16.640
his now famous “one more thing” line. I think&nbsp;
there are a few interesting comparisons between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:21.360
Jobs and Zuckerberg worth discussing here. First&nbsp;
is the redemptive story that Zuck is building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.080
around the Metaverse. He’s been the subject&nbsp;
of intense negative attention for years now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:30.080
and lots of people have been calling for&nbsp;
him to step down. Fortunately for Zuck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.480
he has complete control over the company&nbsp;
and a very supportive board of directors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:39.280
so I don’t think we’ll see a repeat of the Steve&nbsp;
Jobs story play out exactly. But don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:43.280
for a minute that Zuck doesn’t care about what&nbsp;
people think about him. He really does want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:50.800
create products that are adored by users. 91% of&nbsp;
people view Apple favorably, compared to just 71%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:56.560
for Facebook. And so he’s on the hunt for a big&nbsp;
win in the metaverse. It might be VR, it might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:02.960
a social platform, or something else entirely, but&nbsp;
Zuck is clearly restructuring the entire company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:08.000
to be able to try as many ideas as possible. I&nbsp;
think this is a good strategy, but the messaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:13.200
around Zuck’s Meta announcement could not have&nbsp;
been more different than Jobs’ iPhone keynote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:21.120
even though he borrowed the signature “one more&nbsp;
thing”. See, when Steve Jobs announced the iPhone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.920
it was ready to ship. He wasn’t talking about a&nbsp;
far off future where mobile phones were used daily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:30.800
by billions of people. In fact, the first iPhone&nbsp;
didn’t even have an app store. And Apple had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:34.480
incredibly tight-lipped about the fact that they&nbsp;
were working on a phone before the announcement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:38.480
Jobs never mentioned how important mobile was&nbsp;
going to be to Apple until they were ready to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:42.720
launch a polished product. And that’s part of&nbsp;
why it’s so hard to nail down exactly when we’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:47.520
hit the tipping point and be able to confidently&nbsp;
say that we are in the metaverse era. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:52.480
so many different products and companies gradually&nbsp;
improving technology and increasing immersiveness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:57.680
but it might only be clear in retrospect&nbsp;
what the key ingredients were. Even still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:03.040
I think there are some interesting comparisons we&nbsp;
can make to try and project metaverse adoption.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:08.880
First is VR headsets. This year, we should see&nbsp;
about 6 million new sales, which will bring the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:14.560
total number of headsets out in the world to&nbsp;
16 million units. That’s still incredibly low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:20.000
remember, there are billions of internet users.&nbsp;
But the growth is high at 60% per year, and if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:25.280
it continues, we could see one billion headsets&nbsp;
in circulation by 2030. You could also look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:30.480
cryptocurrency adoption rates, since crypto will&nbsp;
likely play a big role in the metaverse long-term.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:35.360
Metamask is the fastest growing ethereum wallet&nbsp;
and a critical part of the defi ecosystem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:39.440
There are millions of people who want nothing&nbsp;
to do with Zuckerberg’s metaverse and will only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:44.960
accept a decentralized option. Metamask usage&nbsp;
is a good proxy for the size of this audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:50.240
and it’s growing even faster than VR. The&nbsp;
wallet had half a million users in July of 2020&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:55.840
and just crossed 10 million users in August of&nbsp;
2021. That works out to an insane 20x annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.840 --> 00:13:00.320
growth rate, which is clearly unsustainable,&nbsp;
but it doesn’t seem crazy to think that we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:04.640
see close to a billion cryptocurrency users&nbsp;
by the end of the decade. There are already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:09.440
100 million bitcoin users after all. Lastly, we&nbsp;
can look at Zuckerberg’s plan specifically. He’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:14.880
aiming to deploy $180 billion dollars over the&nbsp;
next 10 years on the initiative, so he’s obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:20.240
going to have some sort of impact. For reference,&nbsp;
a senior Apple executive said that they spent only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:27.920
$150 million to develop the original iPhone. $180&nbsp;
billion is a staggering amount of money. Truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:33.440
unprecedented in the tech industry. If Meta winds&nbsp;
up spreading that evenly over the next 10 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:40.160
they will be spending fifty times as much money&nbsp;
as the entire Roblox team, every single year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:45.280
Zuckerberg clearly thinks he can have a meaningful&nbsp;
impact in the next 10 years, and has reconfigured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:50.080
his entire business to make something happen.&nbsp;
We still don’t know exactly what the next decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:53.600
will have in store for the metaverse, but&nbsp;
you can be sure that some interesting things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:58.160
will happen here. If you’d like to go a layer&nbsp;
deeper, just drop your email at johncoogan.com,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:08.080
and please check out this recommended video, the&nbsp;
YouTube algorithm thinks you’ll really like it.

